&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:14:47.612 --> 00:14:52.510
&gt;&gt; All right, looks like the next question
may also be for Parker and Lacey,

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:57.250
because I'm seeing a bunch in the Q&amp;A box,
about balancing family and

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:02.330
the very challenging rigorous MBA
academic coursework, course load.

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.511
So Parker as the student,
can you tell us a little bit about how you

00:15:06.511 --> 00:15:10.097
manage your prioritization and
balancing all of that.

00:15:10.097 --> 00:15:13.499
And Lacey,
maybe any other advice you had as well for

00:15:13.499 --> 00:15:17.070
the partner who was married
to a very busy MBA student.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:24.267
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I think one of the driving
forces of nature that sort of runs

00:15:24.267 --> 00:15:29.810
underneath everything in grad school
is FOMO, the fear of missing out.

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:34.713
And so, I think coming into the program,
knowing that we have a family and

00:15:34.713 --> 00:15:39.539
that my schedule typically runs very
different from somebody that maybe

00:15:39.539 --> 00:15:41.600
doesn't have a family.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.545
There's definitely a lot of fear of
missing out on social activities and

00:15:45.545 --> 00:15:49.941
other things because, of course, one of
the main reasons that you do an MBA is for

00:15:49.941 --> 00:15:54.230
developing those relationships and
a network and having those experiences.

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:59.193
So, I think the discipline that was
sort of naturally required was just

00:15:59.193 --> 00:16:03.650
prioritization and
really being thoughtful about

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:07.893
Do I really need to go to a social
event every single night of the week?

00:16:07.893 --> 00:16:11.679
Maybe some weeks, but
probably not most weeks.

00:16:11.679 --> 00:16:15.847
And so I think just being super thoughtful
about what do I actually care about

00:16:15.847 --> 00:16:16.963
participating in?

00:16:16.963 --> 00:16:23.765
And I will say that we were really happy
with our social experiences at the GSB.

00:16:23.765 --> 00:16:28.190
Not just connecting with other families,
but also connecting with all kinds of

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:32.768
different students, single, married,
with kids, without kids, etc, etc.

00:16:32.768 --> 00:16:37.678
And I do think one thing that did help
us is realizing that when you have kids,

00:16:37.678 --> 00:16:42.363
sometimes students that don't have
kids assume that you're too busy or

00:16:42.363 --> 00:16:45.615
maybe you I don't want to
do something socially.

00:16:45.615 --> 00:16:49.769
And so, we took it upon ourselves a little
bit more to try to reach out, and

00:16:49.769 --> 00:16:54.660
invite people over for dinner just so they
knew, hey, yes we have a family, but we're

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:59.233
not so busy with our family that we don't
want to have that relationship with you.

00:16:59.233 --> 00:17:02.012
So we would do it again in a heartbeat.

00:17:02.012 --> 00:17:06.374
Our older kids have friends that they've
made that I think will be lifetime

00:17:06.374 --> 00:17:09.990
friends they met out on
the playground in Escondido village.

00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:14.237
So, yeah,
we would do again in a heartbeat.

00:17:14.237 --> 00:17:16.329
&gt;&gt; So to piggyback on what Parker said,

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:20.510
I think that the biggest thing was
scheduling because there's going to be

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:24.640
a lot of things that your student could
do that they won't do as parents.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:29.431
Because you just can't be gone every
single night until the wee hours

00:17:29.431 --> 00:17:33.155
of the morning like a single
student might be able to.

00:17:33.155 --> 00:17:38.075
Not to say he didn't go to Vegas for
one night thing or whatever, there's still

00:17:38.075 --> 00:17:42.496
opportunity to enjoy that part too,
but you just have to make choices.

00:17:42.496 --> 00:17:45.245
And for us it was, like Parker said,

00:17:45.245 --> 00:17:49.417
inviting people to our house
where our kids felt safe and

00:17:49.417 --> 00:17:55.123
liked meeting different people or
going to campus to meet Parker for lunch.

00:17:55.123 --> 00:17:59.435
So you see all the students and
you make those connections still, but

00:17:59.435 --> 00:18:02.928
in a way that's more family friendly and
more suited for

00:18:02.928 --> 00:18:05.324
the stage of life that you might be in.

00:18:05.324 --> 00:18:06.677
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Lacey.

00:18:06.677 --> 00:18:11.400
Speaking of stages of life and where
you might be in, I would love to turn

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:15.970
things over to Natalya and
Eric because you have a unique perspective

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:20.646
because you have both been students
at the GSB and partners at the GSB.

00:18:20.646 --> 00:18:25.393
So wondering if you can kind of shed some
insights there in terms of some things

00:18:25.393 --> 00:18:29.715
that might be helpful for
folks to consider from either perspective.

00:18:29.715 --> 00:18:33.790
Whether they're students coming with their
partners or what that partner experience

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:36.756
might be like now that you've
seen it from both perspectives.

00:18:36.756 --> 00:18:40.218
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say that if it's possible
if your partner is planning to apply to

00:18:40.218 --> 00:18:42.537
school, definitely do
the exact same thing we did.

00:18:42.537 --> 00:18:46.462
Because then the second person to
go really has a leg up because they

00:18:46.462 --> 00:18:50.473
have the expert in the room and
all social meetups, which is great.

00:18:50.473 --> 00:18:52.728
No, I think it's a great
question Christine, and

00:18:52.728 --> 00:18:55.467
there's probably a lot of different
ways we could go with it.

00:18:55.467 --> 00:18:59.774
I think the first one is really important,
as actually Natalya as already mentioned,

00:18:59.774 --> 00:19:02.966
is deciding whether or
not you're going to be in the same place.

00:19:02.966 --> 00:19:07.658
And that might be other question for other
families on the line, but for us it was

00:19:07.658 --> 00:19:12.576
an important conversation of would, I've
moved to San Francisco or the Bay Area.

00:19:12.576 --> 00:19:16.965
And kind of live either in person or
even just like that middle

00:19:16.965 --> 00:19:20.939
distance between San Francisco and
living on campus.

00:19:20.939 --> 00:19:25.554
And that's ultimately what we did when
she was a student, as I was living in

00:19:25.554 --> 00:19:29.813
San Francisco, and she was living
down in the Bay and we made it work.

00:19:29.813 --> 00:19:33.815
And it kind of kept a little bit
of distance in kids and things.

00:19:33.815 --> 00:19:37.191
So I think that's a really
important question to consider is

00:19:37.191 --> 00:19:39.851
kind of optimizing for
that work life balance.

00:19:39.851 --> 00:19:44.147
And if your partner is interested in
investing more in the community, and

00:19:44.147 --> 00:19:47.958
kind of having kind of a more
individual exposure to their class,

00:19:47.958 --> 00:19:51.009
and really getting some of
those opportunities that

00:19:51.009 --> 00:19:54.434
might not be something that
a working person is keen to do.

00:19:54.434 --> 00:19:57.973
Then that's an important conversation
to have in setting those boundaries and

00:19:57.973 --> 00:19:58.726
expectations.

00:19:58.726 --> 00:20:02.890
&gt;&gt; And I think for us, both of us found
a lot of happiness in having one-on-one

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:05.570
relationships with
the other person's class.

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:08.707
So, for example,
I wasn't relying on my happiness and

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:10.527
my schedule just through Eric.

00:20:10.527 --> 00:20:13.871
I feel like I had individual
relationships with his classmates.

00:20:13.871 --> 00:20:17.123
And that in of itself,
I think makes it a lot easier and

00:20:17.123 --> 00:20:19.944
much more fruitful for
the partner and also for

00:20:19.944 --> 00:20:23.872
the student because then there
is sort of like an independence.

00:20:23.872 --> 00:20:28.621
And it's not fully kind of
a co-dependence as you're kind of

00:20:28.621 --> 00:20:31.282
building your own life on campus.

00:20:31.282 --> 00:20:32.410
&gt;&gt; Thank you both.

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:36.580
Amanda, Tori, I'd love to hear because
I know you've faced similar choices,

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:40.948
relocating from an international location,
figuring out long distance, and

00:20:40.948 --> 00:20:42.006
moving on campus.

00:20:42.006 --> 00:20:45.722
Can you talk to the group a little bit
about how you navigated that process?

00:20:50.165 --> 00:20:52.923
&gt;&gt; I'm happy to go first.

00:20:52.923 --> 00:20:56.715
Gosh, it was an interesting experience.

00:20:56.715 --> 00:21:00.040
I mean, we started doing long distance,

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:04.417
which was really necessary
given the COVID pandemic.

00:21:04.417 --> 00:21:08.921
It was, [LAUGH] so
much choice, it's necessity.

00:21:08.921 --> 00:21:14.222
And that was, we made that work for us.

00:21:14.222 --> 00:21:16.848
We sorted out some rhythms.

00:21:16.848 --> 00:21:20.841
Obviously, we had a very significant
time difference between the two of us.

00:21:20.841 --> 00:21:25.570
I was in Melbourne and
Amanda was in Palo Alto,

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:29.457
but we made it work by putting in time.

00:21:29.457 --> 00:21:33.503
And because some couples may be
thinking about doing long distance or

00:21:33.503 --> 00:21:37.617
may have to do long distance because
of visa reasons for quite a while,

00:21:37.617 --> 00:21:40.007
we put time in each other's calendars.

00:21:40.007 --> 00:21:43.694
Every week that was reserved for
us to hang out with each other.

00:21:43.694 --> 00:21:49.154
Life can be really busy on campus at
Stanford and it's also exciting and

00:21:49.154 --> 00:21:54.257
new, that it can be hard to make time for
other things in your life.

00:21:54.257 --> 00:22:00.308
So we made sure to have some dedicated
time where Amanda wasn't allowed,

00:22:00.308 --> 00:22:04.714
[LAUGH] to go and
do something else, no, I'm joking.

00:22:04.714 --> 00:22:07.456
So we made that work for us.

00:22:07.456 --> 00:22:13.187
We did long distance for,
gosh, five months, I think.

00:22:13.187 --> 00:22:16.050
And then moving on campus,

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:22.161
we spent some time just making
sure that we got settled in.

00:22:22.161 --> 00:22:26.522
And Amanda was really proactive about
making sure that I felt welcomed and

00:22:26.522 --> 00:22:27.233
included.

00:22:27.233 --> 00:22:32.044
Introduced me to a lot of her friends and
I found it quite easy

00:22:32.044 --> 00:22:36.774
to settle into the community actually,
which was great.

00:22:36.774 --> 00:22:40.510
Everyone was super welcoming.

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:44.773
And yeah, we had a great time.

00:22:44.773 --> 00:22:47.851
&gt;&gt; Well, and this is actually
a great segue to ask Amanda,

00:22:47.851 --> 00:22:50.154
you mentioned living in J Mac and Schwab.

00:22:50.154 --> 00:22:51.422
So I am going to go out and

00:22:51.422 --> 00:22:55.171
make a little bit of an assumption
that you might have started at J Mac.

00:22:55.171 --> 00:22:57.452
And then when you were
able to move in together,

00:22:57.452 --> 00:23:00.603
you were able to make the move to
the couple's housing in Schwab.

00:23:00.603 --> 00:23:04.101
So for others who may be dialing in, who
may be facing a similar situation where

00:23:04.101 --> 00:23:07.440
they might have to start long distance,
but there may be an opportunity for

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:08.772
their partner to join later.

00:23:08.772 --> 00:23:12.230
Can you talk a little bit about
how you navigated that process?

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:13.093
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.592
&gt;&gt; I think, Amanda-
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:23:20.592 --> 00:23:21.448
&gt;&gt; Your back.

00:23:21.448 --> 00:23:22.268
Start again.

00:23:22.268 --> 00:23:25.750
&gt;&gt; Sorry, my Internet connection
is not great, so I apologize.

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:26.530
Plan early.

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:31.422
So we knew that Tori was going to arrive
at the start of the winter quarter-ish,

00:23:31.422 --> 00:23:40.257
with, We communicated on,
have I gone off again?

00:23:40.257 --> 00:23:45.797
Yeah, I'm just going to let Tori,
&gt;&gt; I we'll try and

00:23:45.797 --> 00:23:50.889
interpret what Amanda was going,
[LAUGH] to say.

00:23:50.889 --> 00:23:54.727
From what I recall, yes,
we definitely planned early.

00:23:54.727 --> 00:23:56.348
Amanda knew when I was going to arrive,

00:23:56.348 --> 00:24:01.939
so she was in contact with At
the GSB residences and I fell on

00:24:01.939 --> 00:24:07.488
about what rooms might be available
particularly as the terms changed over.

00:24:07.488 --> 00:24:12.460
Because there is some shift in and
out of people in Schwab and J Mac.

00:24:12.460 --> 00:24:19.720
[LAUGH] I hope I'm reading your mind
correctly it's a long distance to read.

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:21.845
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK].

00:24:21.845 --> 00:24:26.560
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So, we actually found
a room in Schwab sometimes, I mean,

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:29.040
the couple rooms very popular.

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:34.486
I wouldn't say that they're
difficult to get when moving in

00:24:34.486 --> 00:24:39.946
because they are kind of
a modification of two single rooms.

00:24:39.946 --> 00:24:42.679
So they can kind of make
couple rooms happen, but

00:24:42.679 --> 00:24:45.420
they need a bit of warning
to to make that happen.

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:48.838
So, we just worked early with
the GSB residences and they were

00:24:48.838 --> 00:24:53.190
able to have a couple's room available for
us when we moved in which was great.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:58.754
&gt;&gt; Now also we did
consider other options so

00:24:58.754 --> 00:25:02.512
we do know lots of partners who do other

00:25:07.584 --> 00:25:09.590
&gt;&gt; Amanda, maybe if you had off video,

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:13.031
we might still be able to catch
your audio we can give that a try.

00:25:19.005 --> 00:25:22.329
All right,
we may have to come back to Amanda there.

00:25:22.329 --> 00:25:24.960
&gt;&gt; Just engaged via chat
&gt;&gt; It's okay [LAUGH]

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:26.603
&gt;&gt; Fantastic please do I'm going to

00:25:26.603 --> 00:25:30.150
switch gears a little bit now thank
you all for your context there.

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:33.721
I think that's very helpful because I know
housing can be something that can make

00:25:33.721 --> 00:25:36.800
folks anxious because there are so
many options.

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:41.777
But for our couples, as I mentioned,
in addition to the Schwab couples housing.

00:25:41.777 --> 00:25:48.020
Couples may also live at Escondido village
or in Mongar so lots of options on campus.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:53.690
Something else that's come up now a couple
of times in q&amp;a are the options for

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:56.210
daycare or childcare on campus.

00:25:56.210 --> 00:26:00.056
So Parker and Lacey, I'm wondering if
that's something that you utilize and

00:26:00.056 --> 00:26:03.156
might be able to speak to or
if you know any classmates that did.

00:26:03.156 --> 00:26:06.791
I don't want to rush around too much
looking for the links on the site, but

00:26:06.791 --> 00:26:10.377
I can try if either of you can maybe
get us started in that conversation.

00:26:13.183 --> 00:26:17.800
&gt;&gt; So I did just post the link I think
it would go through to the daycare.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:21.460
And we did not use the daycare on campus
but we have lots of friends who did.

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:27.183
I will say that when you're moving
to campus it was probably about 50,

00:26:27.183 --> 00:26:29.321
50 of parents that were both working.

00:26:29.321 --> 00:26:31.475
Or parents who may be working before and

00:26:31.475 --> 00:26:34.225
then decided not to just
during that experience.

00:26:34.225 --> 00:26:38.386
Or parents who were just stay at home
parents because that's a decision that

00:26:38.386 --> 00:26:39.240
they had made.

00:26:39.240 --> 00:26:43.280
So there's not really a right or
wrong way to do that.

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:48.811
But the childcare options offered
at Stanford are actually really,

00:26:48.811 --> 00:26:50.880
really great.

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:54.450
I've had some friends claim that they're
the best teachers you can find anywhere

00:26:54.450 --> 00:26:56.500
in the country which might
be the truth [LAUGH].

00:26:56.500 --> 00:27:02.075
And in particular Madeira Grove and
CCSC have had a lot of positive feedback,

00:27:02.075 --> 00:27:05.846
at least what we are that
which was two years ago, but

00:27:05.846 --> 00:27:09.350
I imagine not too much
has changed in that time.

00:27:09.350 --> 00:27:13.835
And specifically, one friend who heard her
two young daughters were there during all

00:27:13.835 --> 00:27:14.774
the COVID stuff.

00:27:14.774 --> 00:27:18.677
And just really impressed with the ways
that they handled all of that for

00:27:18.677 --> 00:27:23.840
young children because that's I mean their
world was shaken, was disrupted too.

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:27.280
And that being said the waitlist
can be quite long so

00:27:27.280 --> 00:27:30.860
if that is something that
you are interested in.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.042
It's a good idea to reach out early and
often.

00:27:33.042 --> 00:27:37.298
And then also during COVID a lot of
people have been using nanny shares.

00:27:37.298 --> 00:27:41.216
And that happened while we were there too
but you just get your Escondido neighbors

00:27:41.216 --> 00:27:44.524
right next door or whatever and
you can just hire somebody together.

00:27:44.524 --> 00:27:47.790
And that was a good solution for
a lot of people.

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:52.839
Somebody asked about the what how
the schools are handling COVID now and

00:27:52.839 --> 00:27:57.142
we are left before COVID hit
we graduated in 2019 [LAUGH].

00:27:57.142 --> 00:28:00.924
But I have heard that kindergarteners and
first graders and

00:28:00.924 --> 00:28:04.740
second graders were back in
person at the end of last year.

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.144
So if you have children older than that,
let me know but

00:28:07.144 --> 00:28:10.775
at least those kids are back in person and
planning to do start again in the fall.

00:28:10.775 --> 00:28:16.567
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Lacey and I did my best to try
to add the the link there in the chat,

00:28:16.567 --> 00:28:19.910
which I think is open to everyone.

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:23.841
This next question is from Rashmi and
I think I'm going to direct this to Eric.

00:28:23.841 --> 00:28:28.330
Because Eric, you mentioned that when you
were the partner, you were actually living

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:31.700
off campus during that experience
living up in San Francisco.

00:28:31.700 --> 00:28:35.778
So Rashmi has a partner who will
need to commute in the South Bay.

00:28:35.778 --> 00:28:38.402
I'm wondering if you can
maybe speak to the pros and

00:28:38.402 --> 00:28:41.040
cons of living off campus
versus living on campus.

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:44.325
And I think now you're in a position
where you've seen it both ways

00:28:44.325 --> 00:28:46.041
&gt;&gt; Yes I can definitely share a few

00:28:46.041 --> 00:28:47.098
thoughts on that.

00:28:47.098 --> 00:28:50.866
So as you mentioned,
I was living in San Francisco and

00:28:50.866 --> 00:28:55.740
Natalya was living on campus and
Schwab and I did not own a car either.

00:28:55.740 --> 00:29:01.100
So I had the double fun time of using many
modes of public transit to get to and

00:29:01.100 --> 00:29:03.490
from where we needed to go.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:07.628
But I will say it is it is quite
doable there is options to it.

00:29:07.628 --> 00:29:11.404
It's very easy to get to the Caltrain
which usually gets you to just about any

00:29:11.404 --> 00:29:14.030
major city that I don't know
where in the South Bay.

00:29:14.030 --> 00:29:15.797
Your partner might be working but

00:29:15.797 --> 00:29:19.846
I can bet that there's probably a caltrain
stop that gets you pretty close.

00:29:19.846 --> 00:29:24.818
And then there's several other options for
public transit if you have a car,

00:29:24.818 --> 00:29:26.587
which is maybe advisable.

00:29:26.587 --> 00:29:29.857
If you are going to be having
a daily commute into work,

00:29:29.857 --> 00:29:31.644
once things start to lift up.

00:29:31.644 --> 00:29:36.865
There's ample parking options on
campus for an additional cost and

00:29:36.865 --> 00:29:40.446
you could drive down to and
from the South Bay.

00:29:40.446 --> 00:29:45.423
Or if you need to commute up
to San Francisco as well but

00:29:45.423 --> 00:29:51.175
I think the obvious downside is
that there's some additional

00:29:51.175 --> 00:29:56.390
commuting to get to campus
if you're living together.

00:29:56.390 --> 00:30:00.506
So that's just something else to keep in
mind is you have multiple cars if you're

00:30:00.506 --> 00:30:03.546
living in Palo Alto,
one of you needs to commute to campus.

00:30:03.546 --> 00:30:07.821
